"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][7]={"school_physics_en:M7-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M7-L1 – Forces as interactions and how to represent them<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Forces, pressure and buoyancy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Push a toy cart and it starts moving. Press a soft sponge and its\nshape changes. Both situations involve a force. Yet “something is\npushed” is not a complete explanation: we need to identify <strong>which\nobject exerts a force on which other object<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>force<\/strong> describes an interaction, for instance\nbetween a hand and a cart or between Earth and a ball. A force may\nchange speed or direction and may deform an object. Forces also act\nwithout visible motion: a book remains on a table even though Earth\npulls it down and the table supports it.<\/p>\n<p>Force is a <strong>vector quantity<\/strong>. A force arrow models its\n<strong>point of application<\/strong>, <strong>line of\naction/direction<\/strong>, <strong>sense<\/strong>, and\n<strong>magnitude<\/strong>. At a defined scale its length represents its\nmagnitude; for example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay stand for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe SI unit is the <strong>newton<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{N}=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStart an arrow on the object <strong>receiving<\/strong> the force in\nyour diagram.<\/p>\n<p><em>Force<\/em> is not a synonym for <em>energy<\/em>. Forces can\ntransfer energy, but newtons and joules measure different\nquantities.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A cart on a horizontal\ntrack with a point of application, a rightward force arrow and a\nscale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_037_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A rightward force arrow starts on a cart; a short scale line represents 2 N.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A rightward force arrow starts on a cart;\na short scale line represents 2 N.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Force arrows represent point of application, direction, sense and\nmagnitude.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A hand pushes a cart horizontally with a force of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the right. With a drawing scale of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\widehat{=}2\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe arrow is <strong>3 cm<\/strong> long and points right. For now we\ndraw <strong>only that one force<\/strong>; we must add all forces before\ninferring the cart’s acceleration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe classroom activity:<\/strong> Hang several small known\nmasses from a suitable spring scale, one at a time, staying below the\ninstrument’s range. Read newtons and sketch the direction of the force.\nDo not jerk the scale or handle heavy weights without supervision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two observable effects of a force.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which four features are represented by a force arrow?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\widehat{=}2\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhow long should an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narrow be?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A cart moves to the right. Does that prove the net force points\nright?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A change in motion and deformation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Point of application, direction/line of action, sense and\nmagnitude.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8/2=4\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. It may move uniformly to the right even if the net force is\nzero.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>A single arrow does not automatically include other forces such as\nfriction and support. A force arrow is a model; real contact forces may\nbe distributed across a surface.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forces, motion, inertia,\nfriction and differentiation in the curriculum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/kraft-und-bewegungsaenderung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Force and changes in motion<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Effects and interactions of forces;\nforce versus motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Lower-secondary physics educational standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanics and\ncompetence domains (2024 standards) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M7-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M7-L2 – Inertia and Newton’s basic laws<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Forces, pressure and buoyancy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A skateboard keeps rolling for some distance after you stop pushing.\nThis does not mean it carries a “stored pushing force”. The observation\ninvites a better explanation of how force is related to changes in\nmotion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Newton’s first law<\/strong>, the law of inertia, applies in\nan inertial frame: if the <strong>sum of external forces is\nzero<\/strong>, an object stays at rest or continues in a\n<strong>straight line at constant velocity<\/strong>. Real objects slow\ndown mainly because of friction and air drag.<\/p>\n<p>For constant mass, <strong>Newton’s second law<\/strong> relates net\nexternal force to acceleration:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec F_{\\mathrm{net}}=m\\vec a.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Acceleration points along the net force, which need not be the\ndirection of the current velocity. Doubling the mass at the same net\nforce halves the acceleration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Newton’s third law<\/strong>, the interaction principle, says\nthat when body A exerts a force on B, B simultaneously exerts an equal\nand opposite force on A. <strong>The two forces act on different\nbodies<\/strong> and therefore do not cancel when summing the forces on\njust one body. That is the key distinction between interaction pairs and\nequilibrium.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A cart with zero net\nforce compared with a cart experiencing a net pull, distinguishing\nvelocity from acceleration arrows.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_038_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Equal opposite pulls balance in the upper panel; a nonzero net pull and rightward acceleration are shown below.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Equal opposite pulls balance in the upper\npanel; a nonzero net pull and rightward acceleration are shown\nbelow.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Velocity does not have to point along force; net force determines\nacceleration.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncart experiences a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npull to the right and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfriction to the left. Net force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the right. Its acceleration is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\frac{F_{\\mathrm{net}}}{m}=\\frac{3\\,\\mathrm{N}}{2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}}=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>to the right. Whether it was already moving does not change that\ninstantaneous acceleration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> Roll a small wheeled toy across two\nlevel surfaces with different friction. Compare its motion after\nrelease; prevent it from rolling towards anyone. A smoother surface\nreduces deceleration but does not create an ideal friction-free\nsystem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to an already moving object if net force is zero in an\ninertial frame?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find acceleration if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=3\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{net}}=6\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do a Newton’s-third-law pair of forces not cancel on the\ncart?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A car moves right but slows down. Which direction is its net\nforce?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It continues along a straight line at constant velocity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=6/3=2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the direction of net force.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because the action–reaction forces act on different objects.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Opposite the motion, here to the left.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Here we work in approximately inertial frames with objects of\nconstant mass. Rotating or accelerating frames require further\ntreatment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forces, motion, inertia,\nfriction and differentiation in the curriculum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/kraft-und-bewegungsaenderung/grundwissen/1-newtonsches-gesetz-traegheitsgesetz\">LEIFIphysik\n– Newton’s first law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Inertia and constant velocity when net force\nvanishes · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/kraft-und-bewegungsaenderung/grundwissen/wechselwirkung-ungleich-gleichgewicht\">LEIFIphysik\n– Interaction is not equilibrium<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Newton III: action–reaction\nforces act on different objects · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Lower-secondary physics educational standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanics and\ncompetence domains (2024 standards) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M7-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M7-L3 – Adding forces and force equilibrium<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Forces, pressure and buoyancy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two people pull the same sled in opposite directions. A change in\nvelocity depends not on one pull alone but on the vector sum of\n<strong>all<\/strong> forces exerted on the sled.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Forces are vectors. To add forces along a straight axis, first define\na positive direction. Then the <strong>net force<\/strong> obeys<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{net},x}=F_{1,x}+F_{2,x}+\\dots.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A rightward force is positive; a leftward one is negative. A positive\nsum means net force to the right, while a negative sum means left.\nEqual, opposite forces on <strong>the same object<\/strong> give zero net\nforce: <strong>force equilibrium<\/strong>. This permits rest\n<strong>or<\/strong> constant straight-line velocity.<\/p>\n<p>For forces acting in different directions, add components as vectors.\nWith perpendicular components, for example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\vec F_{\\mathrm{net}}|=\\sqrt{F_x^2+F_y^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdding magnitudes alone would give the wrong result. The sled’s reaction\nforce <strong>on the person<\/strong> is not part of the sled’s own force\ndiagram.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Two pairs of horizontal\nforces illustrating zero and nonzero resultant arrows.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_039_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two carts show opposing 6 N and 4 N forces giving a +2 N resultant, and equal 4 N forces in equilibrium.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two carts show opposing 6 N and 4 N\nforces giving a +2 N resultant, and equal 4 N forces in\nequilibrium.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Add forces on the same body; zero net force also permits uniform\nmotion.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Forces along a sled’s track are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_1=+6\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_2=-4\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe resultant is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{net},x}=6\\,\\mathrm{N}-4\\,\\mathrm{N}=+2\\,\\mathrm{N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Acceleration is rightwards. An <strong>extra<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nforce would establish equilibrium. Even so, a sled originally moving\nleft could first slow down, stop and then reverse direction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe classroom activity:<\/strong> Attach two spring scales to\na light ring on a level surface and pull with equal strength in opposite\ndirections. Observe the stationary ring, then carefully change one pull.\nStay within the scales’ ranges and never suddenly release a stretched\nspring.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+5\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-8\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What additional force balances a resultant of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+3\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can an object be moving while in force equilibrium? Explain.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the magnitude of two perpendicular forces of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmeaning leftward.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nleftward.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes. In an inertial frame it may keep moving straight at constant\nvelocity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Always assign the diagrammed forces to one chosen object. Forces\napplied at different places can also create a turning effect even when\ntheir vector sum is zero.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forces, motion, inertia,\nfriction and differentiation in the curriculum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/kraefteaddition-und-zerlegung/grundwissen/kraefteaddition\">LEIFIphysik\n– Vector addition of forces<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Net force obtained by vector addition\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/kraft-und-kraftarten/grundwissen/gleichgewicht-von-kraeften-einfuehrung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Introduction to force equilibrium<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Balanced forces on an object,\nrest or constant velocity · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Lower-secondary physics educational standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanics and\ncompetence domains (2024 standards) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M7-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M7-L4 – Mass, weight and gravitational field strength<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Forces, pressure and buoyancy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>An astronaut takes the same tool case to the Moon as on Earth. The\ncase has less <strong>weight<\/strong>, but not less\n<strong>mass<\/strong>. Why does a spring scale change its reading while\na properly used balance does not?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Mass<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nis a measure related to inertia and is expressed in kilograms\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\n<strong>Weight<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nis the gravitational force acting on an object. Near a planet’s or\nmoon’s surface we use<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G=m\\,g.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Near Earth’s surface, gravitational field strength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile on the Moon it is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.62<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.62\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}=1\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe can also express the magnitude of free-fall acceleration in these\nunits when other influences are neglected.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>spring scale<\/strong> measures force. An ideal <strong>beam\nbalance<\/strong> compares masses: the same local gravitational field\nacts on both sides. Everyday speech sometimes calls mass “weight”, but\nthe two concepts differ in physics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> The same case on Earth\nand the Moon, with identical mass labels but different-sized weight\narrows.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_040_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two 3 kg boxes on Earth and the Moon carry differently sized arrows for gravitational force.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two 3 kg boxes on Earth and the Moon\ncarry differently sized arrows for gravitational force.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Mass remains unchanged; weight depends on local gravitational\nfield strength.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A box has mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=3.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNear Earth’s surface its weight is approximately<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>29.43<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{G,E}=3.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\times9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}=29.43\\,\\mathrm{N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>On the Moon,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1.62<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.86<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{G,M}=3.0\\times1.62=4.86\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts mass stays\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin either place.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> With supervision, measure the weights\nof small, securely attached masses using a spring scale. Plot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand estimate local gravitational field strength from the slope. Stay\nbelow the instrument’s stated load limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which stays the same when moving an object from Earth to the Moon:\nmass or weight?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the SI unit of weight as a force?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why would a spring scale calibrated to show mass on the Moon give\ndifferent readings on Earth?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mass stays the same; weight changes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.905<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.91<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G=0.50\\times9.81=4.905\\,\\mathrm{N}\\approx4.91\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The newton (N).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It measures force, and gravitational field strength differs between\nthe two locations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The stated field strengths are approximations. Position, altitude and\nplanetary rotation slightly affect locally measured effective gravity. A\nfreely falling scale may read zero even while gravity acts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forces, motion, inertia,\nfriction and differentiation in the curriculum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/kraft-und-masse-ortsfaktor/grundwissen/gewichtskraft\">LEIFIphysik\n– Weight as gravitational force<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Distinction between mass, weight\nand gravitational field strength · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Lower-secondary physics educational standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanics and\ncompetence domains (2024 standards) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M7-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M7-L5 – Friction and drag<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Forces, pressure and buoyancy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A book stays put on a slightly tilted board. Increase the angle and\nit eventually slides. Friction is therefore not a single fixed opposing\nforce: it adapts up to a limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>At the contact between two surfaces, <strong>static friction<\/strong>\nprevents relative slipping. It adjusts to the required magnitude up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{s,\\max}=\\mu_s F_N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit is <strong>not always equal<\/strong> to this maximum. Once sliding\noccurs, the simple school model uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_k\\approx\\mu_k F_N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the normal force and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a dimensionless coefficient of friction.<\/p>\n<p>For a body on an inclined plane at angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nweight and contact forces act <strong>on that same body<\/strong>. With\nno other perpendicular forces,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo>∥<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{G,\\parallel}=m g\\sin\\alpha,\\qquad F_N=m g\\cos\\alpha.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the object remains still, static friction opposes its downhill\nweight component and matches it exactly, <strong>provided<\/strong> the\nmaximum static friction is large enough. Once it moves, kinetic friction\ncan be smaller. <strong>Air drag<\/strong>, in contrast, is due to\ninteraction with a fluid and depends on factors such as shape and speed.\nIt does not generally follow the sliding-friction formula.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A block on an incline,\nshowing gravity, the normal force, static friction and the downhill\ngravity component.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_041_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A block on an incline has separate arrows for weight, normal force, static friction and downhill component of weight.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A block on an incline has separate arrows\nfor weight, normal force, static friction and downhill component of\nweight.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Static friction opposes impending downhill motion if its limit is\nsufficient.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nblock rests on an incline of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo>∥<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.905<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{G,\\parallel}=1.0\\times9.81\\times\\sin30^\\circ=4.905\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>downhill; the normal force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_N=9.81\\cos30^\\circ\\approx8.50\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the block stays still, static friction is <strong>4.905 N\nuphill<\/strong>; this is possible only if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>4.905<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8.50<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.577<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_s\\geq4.905/8.50\\approx0.577<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> Place a small wooden block on a\nfirmly supported tilting board. Increase the angle slowly and record the\nangle at which sliding begins. Cushion the end of the board; avoid heavy\nobjects or unstable setups.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do static and kinetic friction differ?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a stationary object, must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalways equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_sF_N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which way does static friction act on a block that would otherwise\nslide down an incline?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Static friction prevents slipping up to a limit; kinetic friction\nacts during sliding.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; static friction takes the necessary value up to its\nmaximum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19.62<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_N=m g=19.62\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Up the incline.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Constant friction coefficients are an idealisation; moisture, surface\ncondition and speed affect real friction. The incline equations apply to\nthe stated force situation without extra perpendicular forces.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forces, motion, inertia,\nfriction and differentiation in the curriculum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/reibung-und-fortbewegung/grundwissen/haft-gleit-und-rollreibung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Static, kinetic and rolling friction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Friction types and\napplicability of simple models · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/kraefteaddition-und-zerlegung/grundwissen/kraefte-der-schiefen-ebene-rechnerisch\">LEIFIphysik\n– Forces on an inclined plane<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Resolving weight into parallel and\nnormal components · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Lower-secondary physics educational standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanics and\ncompetence domains (2024 standards) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M7-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M7-L6 – Pressure in solids, liquids and gases<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Forces, pressure and buoyancy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A snowshoe carries the same weight as a narrow heel but usually sinks\nmuch less into soft snow. Force alone does not explain the outcome: the\narea over which it acts matters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Pressure<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis perpendicular force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\perp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper unit area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><mi>A<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\frac{F_\\perp}{A},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{N/m^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the simple model of uniform contact, a larger area under the same\nforce gives a smaller average contact pressure. Pressure in a liquid at\nrest acts in all directions on surrounding surfaces, with the force on\nany particular surface perpendicular to it. For a liquid of\napproximately constant density, the <strong>additional hydrostatic\npressure<\/strong> at depth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelow the surface is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p=\\rho g h.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis density in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis gravitational field strength and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis depth. To find <strong>absolute pressure<\/strong> in open water, also\ninclude atmospheric pressure at the surface. Gases exert pressure too;\nbecause they are more compressible, the constant-density formula cannot\nbe extended unchanged over large altitude differences.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> One block on differently\nsized contact faces alongside a water column labelled at increasing\ndepths.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_042_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"The same load acts on two different contact areas, while pressure arrows grow with depth in a water column.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">The same load acts on two different\ncontact areas, while pressure arrows grow with depth in a water\ncolumn.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Pressure is force per area; additional hydrostatic pressure\nincreases with depth.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>An area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.020\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncarries a perpendicular force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=200\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAverage pressure is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\frac{200\\,\\mathrm{N}}{0.020\\,\\mathrm{m^2}}=10\\,000\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=10\\,\\mathrm{kPa}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For fresh water with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho\\approx1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe additional pressure at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>620<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>19.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p=1000\\times9.81\\times2.0=19\\,620\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\approx19.6\\,\\mathrm{kPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is <strong>not<\/strong> the complete absolute pressure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> Press the same small rectangular\nblock gently into modelling clay, first on its largest and then on a\nsmaller face. Compare the marks. A supervised transparent water column\ncan demonstrate hydrostatic pressure; do not build pressurised vessels\nor use breakable glass apparatus.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find pressure for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=60\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.030\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a larger contact area lower average contact pressure?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What must be added to additional hydrostatic pressure to find\nabsolute pressure in open water?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For unchanged liquid, what happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p=\\rho gh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2000\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The same force is distributed across a greater area, reducing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F/A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Pressure at the surface, normally atmospheric pressure.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Additional hydrostatic pressure doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Hydrostatic pressure here assumes nearly constant density and\ngravitational field. Pressure across a real solid contact surface may be\nnonuniform; the simple model uses an average.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L7<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forces, motion, inertia,\nfriction and differentiation in the curriculum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/druck-und-auftrieb/grundwissen/druck\">LEIFIphysik\n– Pressure<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Definition of pressure as force per area · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/druck-und-auftrieb/grundwissen/schweredruck\">LEIFIphysik\n– Hydrostatic pressure<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hydrostatic pressure as additional pressure\ndepending on depth · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Lower-secondary physics educational standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanics and\ncompetence domains (2024 standards) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M7-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M7-L7 – Buoyancy and floating<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Forces, pressure and buoyancy<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A ball of modelling clay sinks in water, but a boat shaped from the\nsame clay can float. The amount of clay has not changed. What matters is\nhow much water is displaced and how the forces on the object\ncompare.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>An object immersed in a fluid (liquid or gas) experiences an upward\n<strong>buoyant force<\/strong>. <strong>Archimedes’ principle<\/strong>\nstates that its magnitude equals the weight of fluid displaced:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_B=\\rho_{\\mathrm{fluid}}\\,g\\,V_{\\mathrm{displaced}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a fully submerged object, displaced volume is usually its total\nimmersed volume. For a floating object, only the <strong>submerged\npart<\/strong> counts. The object’s weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G=mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacts downward.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Sinking:<\/strong> If the weight of a fully submerged object\nexceeds buoyancy (ignoring other forces here), it initially accelerates\ndownwards.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Neutral buoyancy:<\/strong> A fully immersed object with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_B=F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis in equilibrium in the simple model and may remain at a given\ndepth.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Floating:<\/strong> An object at the surface displaces enough\nfluid that, when at rest,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_B=F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is therefore only partly immersed. A body that is accelerating\nupwards may first <strong>rise<\/strong>; this is not the same as\nfloating at the surface.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The clay boat’s shape encloses air and displaces more water for the\nsame overall mass than a compact lump. <strong>Original teaching\ndiagram:<\/strong> Sinking, neutrally buoyant and floating objects with\nseparate upward buoyancy and downward weight arrows.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_043_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three vessels illustrate sinking, neutral buoyancy and floating with weight and buoyancy arrows.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three vessels illustrate sinking, neutral\nbuoyancy and floating with weight and buoyancy arrows.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The relative size of weight and buoyancy explains the behaviour\nin water.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A fully immersed object displaces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0.0020\\,\\mathrm{m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof fresh water. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0.0020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19.62<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_B=1000\\times9.81\\times0.0020\\,\\mathrm{N}=19.62\\,\\mathrm{N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If its mass is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits weight is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14.715<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G=14.715\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBuoyancy initially wins when fully immersed. At the surface the object\ncan remain afloat at the <strong>smaller displaced volume<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0015<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{displaced}}=m/\\rho=0.0015\\,\\mathrm{m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided its orientation is stable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe activity:<\/strong> Shape the same amount of modelling\nclay into a compact ball and then into a broad, reasonably watertight\nbowl. Lower each gently into a shallow water tray on a nonslip surface\nand compare floating behaviour. Keep water away from electrical\nequipment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What determines buoyant force in the Archimedes model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate buoyancy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{L}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof displaced water with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompare for an object floating at rest?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a clay boat more likely to float than a clay ball of the same\nmass?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Fluid density, gravitational field strength and displaced fluid\nvolume.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{L}=0.001\\,\\mathrm{m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_B=9.81\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_B=F_G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen other vertical forces are negligible.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By its shape and enclosed air space, it can displace enough water\nbefore submerging completely to balance its weight.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The model assumes fluids at rest. Fluid flow, surface tension,\ntrapped air, changing density and floating stability may also affect\nreal situations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The following module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1 – Forms of energy and energy\ntransfer<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forces, motion, inertia,\nfriction and differentiation in the curriculum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/druck-und-auftrieb/grundwissen/auftriebskraft\">LEIFIphysik\n– Buoyant force<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Archimedes’ principle and the weight of displaced\nfluid · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/druck-und-auftrieb/grundwissen/schweredruck\">LEIFIphysik\n– Hydrostatic pressure<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hydrostatic pressure as additional pressure\ndepending on depth · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Lower-secondary physics educational standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanics and\ncompetence domains (2024 standards) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
